{
  "_about": "EDTF (ISO 8601-2:2019 Annex A) conformance corpus — the parser-agnostic ORACLE.",
  "_purpose": "The single source of truth for 'what is valid EDTF and what does it mean'. Both candidate parsers (A = plpgsql, B = plv8/edtf.js) are graded against this exact corpus, so they cannot diverge silently. Drives the pgTAP conformance seedtest. Drawn from every ISO 8601-2:2019 Annex A example (read 2026-06-22) + adversarial cases for the known regex-pile bug classes.",
  "_field_notes": {
    "edtf": "the input string",
    "level": "0 | 1 | 2 — EDTF profile conformance level the feature belongs to (Annex A); 'x' = invalid",
    "valid": "true if a conformant EDTF parser MUST accept it",
    "earliest": "expected lower date bound as YYYY-MM-DD; null = unknown (open-empty '/'); '-infinity'/'infinity' = open-double-dot '..'; omitted where bounds are out of scope of this row's assertion",
    "latest": "expected upper date bound",
    "precision": "year|month|day|datetime|decade|century|season|interval|set|list|null",
    "type": "date|datetime|interval|set|list|season",
    "note": "human description / why it's here"
  },
  "_bounds_conventions": "Bounds are date-typed (range 4713 BC – 5874897 AD). EDTF years outside that (exponential 'Y..E..', deep pre-4713-BC) validate but bound to null (lossless string is the SoT). Open interval end '..' -> infinity/-infinity; unknown end '' -> null. These bound expectations are the design target; rows whose bounds depend on an OPEN sub-decision carry \"bounds_open\": true.",

